-- title:
--   Module of an invertible ideal sheaf: invertibility and function-field presentation
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be an integral scheme and let $I$ be a quasi-coherent ideal sheaf datum on $X$ satisfying `IsInvertible`: every point of $X$ lies in $X.basicOpen f$ for some affine open $U$ and some $f \in \Gamma(X,U)$ such that $I$ evaluated on the affine open $X.affineBasicOpen f$ is the ideal generated by a single non-zero-divisor of $\Gamma(X, X.affineBasicOpen f)$. Then two things hold. First, the $\mathcal O_X$-module $I.module$, defined as the kernel of the canonical map from the unit module $\mathcal O_X$ to the pushforward of the unit module along the closed immersion $I.subschemeι$, is invertible in the sense that each point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ on which the pullback of $I.module$ along $U.\iota$ is isomorphic to the unit module of $U$. Second, there is a family of additive maps $\varphi_V \colon \Gamma(I.module, V) \to K$, for all opens $V \subseteq X$, where $K$ is the function field of $X$, such that: $\varphi_{V'}$ applied to the restriction of $m$ along $V' \le V$ equals $\varphi_V(m)$ whenever $V'$ is non-empty; $\varphi_V(a \cdot m) = \iota(a)\,\varphi_V(m)$ for $a \in \Gamma(X,V)$ and non-empty $V$, where $\iota$ is the structure map $\Gamma(X,V) \to K$; each $\varphi_V$ with $V$ non-empty is injective; and for every non-empty affine open $W$ and every non-zero-divisor $g \in \Gamma(X,W)$ with $I$ evaluated on $W$ equal to $(g)$, there is a section $m \in \Gamma(I.module, W)$ which is a frame on $W$, i.e. for every open $W' \le W$ the map $\Gamma(X,W') \to \Gamma(I.module, W')$, $a \mapsto a \cdot (m|_{W'})$, is bijective, and which satisfies $\varphi_W(m) =$ the germ of $g$ in $K$.
--
--   This packages the standard identification of the ideal sheaf of an effective Cartier divisor on an integral scheme with an invertible module embedded in the constant sheaf with value the function field, the local generators serving as frames. It is the form in which invertibility and the function-field presentation are used in the construction of models of modular curves, being cited by [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP.exists_isInvertible_presentation_frames_slopeLaw_fixed_base_strict_of_dvd_width`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP.exists_isInvertible_presentation_frames_slopeLaw_fixed_base_strict_of_dvd_width); invertibility of `I.module` itself is quoted from [`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsInvertible.isInvertible_module`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsInvertible.isInvertible_module).
